---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PawIcon from '../components/PawIcon.astro'

// The editorial-standards page. Every article byline links here, and so does
// the footer.
//
// This page exists to answer, in specific terms, the question a careful reader
// (or an ad-network reviewer) actually has: where do these picks come from, and
// what is this site not doing? Vague reassurance is worse than useless here —
// the old About page claimed picks were checked against "testing data from
// sources that actually put products through the wringer", which sounded like a
// lab and wasn't one. Everything below is meant to be literally true. If the
// process changes, change this page first.
const updated = 'September 2026'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How we pick — Shopping Sloth"
  description="Our editorial process in plain terms: where picks come from, how AI is used, what we don't do, and how affiliate links work."
>
  <div class="wrap prose" style="max-width:760px;">
    <div class="page-head">
      <div class="eyebrow">
        <PawIcon size={12} />
        editorial standards
      </div>
      <h1>How we pick</h1>
    </div>

    <p class="lede">
      Short version: we read what the people who actually test products have published, and we turn that
      into a shortlist. We don't test anything ourselves, and we don't pretend otherwise.
    </p>

    <p>Last updated {updated}.</p>

    <h2>Where the picks come from</h2>
    <p>
      Every roundup starts with published testing and review coverage from outlets that put products
      through real evaluation — places like Consumer Reports, Wirecutter, Rtings, CNET, Treeline Review,
      NBC Select, and CNN Underscored. A product makes our shortlist when independent reviewers who
      handled it rated it well, and when its purchase and review history suggests it holds up past the
      first few weeks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sources consulted for each roundup are listed at the bottom of that article, so you can go
      check the underlying coverage rather than taking our word for it. If a category doesn't have
      enough well-reviewed products to fill a list, we'd rather publish a shorter one than pad it.
    </p>

    <h2>How AI is used, and where it stops</h2>
    <p>
      We draft with AI. Specifically: we use Anthropic's Claude API, with web search, to research a
      category and produce a first draft grounded in current published reviews. This is a small
      independent site and that's how we cover ground.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing publishes itself. Every draft lands in our CMS as an unpublished draft and a person reads
      it before it goes live — checking that the products named are real and currently sold, that the
      reasoning matches what the cited sources actually say, and that nothing has drifted into claims we
      can't support. Drafts that don't survive that check get fixed or dropped.
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>We don't test products.</strong> There's no lab, no test bench, no sample closet. When
        you read that something "runs quiet" here, that's a finding from published testing, not from us
        standing next to it with a decibel meter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We don't quote prices.</strong> They change constantly and we can't verify them at the
        moment you're reading. Check the retailer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We don't take payment for placement.</strong> No brand has ever paid to appear on this
        site, to be ranked higher, or to be named editor's pick. If that ever changes, it will be
        labeled on the page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We don't invent products or specs.</strong> Product names are the names they're sold
        under. If we can't confirm a detail, it doesn't go in.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-we-make-money">How we make money</h2>
    <p>
      Shopping Sloth is an affiliate site. Some product links are affiliate links, and as an Amazon
      Associate we earn from qualifying purchases made through them, at no extra cost to you. We also
      run display advertising.
    </p>
    <p>
      Commissions don't influence the rankings. The practical reason is simple: the commission rate is
      roughly the same across the products we cover, so there's nothing to gain by steering you toward a
      worse one — and a reader who buys something disappointing doesn't come back. Every article carries
      this disclosure above its first affiliate link, not buried at the bottom.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping things current</h2>
    <p>
      Product roundups go stale — models get discontinued, revised, or quietly made worse. Each article
      shows when it was last updated. We revisit roundups as categories change, and when a pick is
      discontinued we replace it rather than leaving a dead recommendation up.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If something here is wrong — a product that no longer exists, a claim that doesn't match the
      source, a broken link — tell us at
      <a href="mailto:hello@shoppingsloth.com">hello@shoppingsloth.com</a> and we'll fix it and note the
      update date on the article. We'd genuinely rather hear it than not.
    </p>

    <div class="quote" style="margin:56px 0;">
      <p>"We move slow, but only toward the good stuff."</p>
      <span>— shopping sloth</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
